Reporting standards
Each published benchmark should report:

library and version;
data dimensions;
hardware;
compiler and build type;
thread count;
elapsed time;
peak memory;
numerical agreement or tolerance, where relevant;
the exact command or script used.

Intended areas

packed genotype operations;
genomic matrix and sparse-LD operations;
numerical solvers;
ML/REML inference;
LD-score regression, where relevant;
genomic scoring.

Benchmark reports should distinguish method validation from performance qualification and should make comparisons reproducible from the information provided.

Code belongs in gbase only when it is stable, task-neutral and needed by more than one task package. The package contains or will own:

allele-labelled summary-statistics data frames, marker-by-trait matrices and gact Z/N lists;
checkStat() mapping, harmonization and explicit QC of reported statistics against a Glist reference;
opt-in DENTIST-style LD consistency checks and quality-filtered Z-score imputation against ancestry-matched complete regional BED correlation;
validation of marker identity, positions, alleles and supplied numerical columns;
explicit allele-oriented variant keys;
gbase::gprep() preparation and sparse-LD publication plus the serializable Glist contract shared by individual-level task packages;
gbase::define_ld_blocks() one-time verification and serialization of an exhaustive independent-block partition tied to the sparse-LD identity;
summary-based marker filtering and Glist-owned LD scores, regional matrices, complete verified regional eigensystems, and reusable eigenvalues-only full regional BED spectra through gprep(task = "eigen", sets = ...).

checkStat() follows the established qgg workflow and preserves ordinary input formats. Its data-frame columns are rsids, chr, pos, ea, nea, with the available analysis columns among eaf, b, seb, stat, p, n and info. Downstream functions validate the subset they require. Gbase does not require a public summary-statistics class, constructor, accessor or print method.
The following do not belong in gbase:

automatic strand inference or downstream LD-model fitting;
genotype, LD or matrix storage;
statistical fitting, priors or sampler controls;
scoring, correlation or association algorithms;
orchestration across tasks.

Those responsibilities remain with gmat/data preparation, the relevant task package or the gsuite umbrella.

Current public headers and R exports define what is implemented. Focused tests and committed qualification evidence determine whether a specific model/backend/inference combination is qualified. The catalog uses exactly:

qualified: implemented, publicly reachable where claimed, and covered by focused numerical or contract evidence for that combination;
implemented_unqualified: code exists, but the complete claimed combination lacks focused qualification;
unsupported: the combination rejects explicitly or has no implementation;
not_applicable: the concept does not apply to the route.

Code presence alone never establishes qualified. A limitation on one route must not be generalized to the suite.

Dominance and hybrid combining ability
relationships:
  D: {type: dominance, genotypes: genotypes, independent_ridge: 0.05}
The native genotype kernel counts BIM allele 1. With allele frequency \(p\), dominance scores for dosages 0, 1 and 2 are \(-2p^2\), \(2p(1-p)\) and \(-2(1-p)^2\). The Gram matrix is divided by \(\sum[2p(1-p)]^2\) before the explicit ridge is added. Frequencies are estimated from nonmissing FAM samples; missing calls contribute zero coded value and monomorphic markers are omitted. This population-orthogonal coding follows Vitezica, Varona and Legarra (2013) under Hardy-Weinberg assumptions. It does not guarantee sample orthogonality. Additive predictions remain breeding values; additive plus dominance predictions represent a total genotypic contribution. Dominance is a separate effect on the panel individuals; it does not create a single-step dominance pedigree model. See the dominance example (models/multitrait-dominance.yml).
relationships:
  A1: {type: covariance, input: pool1}
  A2: {type: covariance, input: pool2}
  S: {type: cross_product, parent1: A1, parent2: A2, crosses: crosses}
For ordered crosses \((a,b)\) and \((c,d)\), \(S_{(a,b),(c,d)}=A1_{a,c}A2_{b,d}\). The crosses table supplies unique IDs and ordered parent pairs. Parent relationships currently use supplied dense natural covariance matrices. Separate parent GCA effects and a cross SCA effect use the existing random-effect incidence mappings. The model is the sum of those contributions; no unrequested one-half coefficient is inserted. See the hybrid GCA/SCA example (models/hybrid-gca-sca.yml).
Both relationship preparations currently produce a dense covariance and use the compiled relationship factor. They are not matrix-free industrial dominance or cross-product operators. Dominance decoding uses bounded marker tiles; kernel storage is quadratic in selected individuals/crosses. Optional per-relationship maximum_workspace_bytes bounds kernel construction, not total process RSS or the consuming factor. The default introduces no fixed memory ceiling. Existing runner timings, memory reporting and result collection apply.
